 ## Acceptance criteria
 
 ### Checked
-<!-- ACn: statement. Each maps 1:1 to a `check "ACn: ..."` line in check.sh. Deterministic. -->
+- AC1: z-nile's skills live in `z-nile/skills/<name>/`, a project's in `<project>/skills/<name>/`, and `./zn skills` makes `.claude/skills/<name>` a relative link to each. It removes wrong and extra links and refuses a real folder there. `--check` writes nothing and names every missing, extra, wrong or non-link entry and every tracked non-link. Both refuse a folder without `SKILL.md`, a `name:` that is not its folder's, a name two folders share, a z-nile skill outside `z-`/`zz-`, and a project skill not named `<project>-…`. Writing refuses in the main checkout. Both refuse a linked `.claude` or `.claude/skills`, and `--check` names a tracked `.claude/skills` itself.
+- AC2: in a plain clone of HEAD under `.cache/c0115-ac2` (never `git worktree add`), `./zn skills --check` prints its ok line and `z-nile/check.sh --plan` its `skills:` line, and the clone's `git status --porcelain` stays empty; and the real `z-nile/check.sh` runs the stage (its exact line).
+- AC3: the eleven old skills are renamed as §1 says, nothing old is left in any skills folder or in `.claude/skills`, `.claude/skills` is one committed link per skill folder, `.claude/README.md` is gone, every architecture lesson is zz-arch's (the four the prototype has are pinned), z-design has three checklists, and no `z-raft` is made.
+- AC4: no live file names an old skill in any of §8's forms or cites an old lesson id whose renamed lesson exists, and no code or spec names a path through a `.claude/skills` link.
+- AC5: lessons are read from the real skill folders (an old tree reads `.claude/skills`), ids use the new names, `--full` prints real paths, one skill in two folders is a problem, and no ledger or lesson on the real tree names an old id.
+- AC6: the real tree's `./zn lessons --check` passes, and `./zn lessons --uncovered R0040 R0042` finds every finding covered.
+- AC7: a raft's prompt inlines `z-run`, `zz-rules`, `zz-arch`, `zz-test` and `zz-review` in that order, then its own project's skills, from their real folders, with the lessons of z-nile's and its own project's skills only, and names its project.
+- AC8: the skills, AGENTS.md, SETUP.md, the usage text, the board's commands and the engine's messages say what §1 to §8 say, with the new names.
+- AC9: `z-nile/README.md` opens with a mermaid picture of the tracker, the skills, the dispatcher, `.models.toml` and the board; every skill it draws exists or is labelled with its issue and `planned`; and it names every skill folder and the three checklists.
+- AC10: the repository-fence, structure, lessons-paragraph and check-path specs pass in both homes.
+- AC11: `./zn audit` reports no error on the real tree.
+- AC12: as of the last fetch, `./zn busy .claude/skills z-nile/skills pickman/skills` prints nothing on the raft branch.
+- AC13: the branch's commits since it left `origin/main` hold exactly one commit whose subject starts `c0115: move the skills`, its `git show -M --find-renames=100% --name-status` lines are all `R100`, and no other commit of the range renames a file under `.claude/skills`, `z-nile/skills` or `pickman/skills`. It is a branch check (`./zn check`, `done`), like c0114's AC13; after the squash merge its range is empty.
 
 ### Judged
-<!-- Only if genuinely subjective. JCn: statement + rubric (what pass/fail looks like).
-     Ruled on by the adversarial code reviewer. -->
+- JC1: the PR is reviewable as a rename. The mechanics are AC13; the reviewer judges only whether the edits commit reads as edits of moved files (`git diff -M` shows each changed skill as a modification at its new path, never as a delete and an add). Fail when it doesn't.
+- JC2: the README is a short, true map. Rubric: pass when `z-nile/README.md`'s account of the five parts is at most about 15 lines, says nothing an existing skill contradicts (each sentence checked against zz-rules, zz-test and z-run), and marks every unbuilt part with its issue; fail on a stale command or path, a part described as built that is not, or rules copied out of a skill rather than pointed at.
 
